<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AssemblyName>vocabulary-check</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>DoricoUse.VocabularyCheck</RootNamespace>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <!--
      B2: same bar as the CLI - the house style and the analyzers are enforced here too, and a warning fails the
      build, because a check that is allowed to warn stops being read.
    -->
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The check compiles the vocabulary's own sources instead of referencing the CLI: the CLI is an executable with a
    top-level Program. It needs nothing else - no Dorico, no host, no extracted file, and no Python: the table is
    built in, which is the whole point of the check.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/CliException.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Dorico/EnumVocabulary.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Dorico/NoteValue.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Dorico/NodeRead.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Dorico/DoricoProtocol.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Dorico/UnsafeSurface.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Json/JsonArr.cs" />
    <!--
      The dialog model is pure data and pure rules: no Win32, no UI Automation, nothing Windows-shaped, which is
      exactly why it can be asserted here on net10.0 without a dialog. The Windows side of the feature lives under
      Ui/ as well, in files only the CLI project compiles.
    -->
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Ui/DialogModel.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Ui/DialogCatalogue.cs" />
    <!--
      The dialog memory is the same kind of file: a JSON file under %LOCALAPPDATA%\dorico-use, a clock, a predicate
      the Windows side supplies as a function, and no Win32 anywhere. It is asserted here with a file of the check's
      own and a clock the check passes in, because "the same handle keeps its firstSeen" is a rule about time and no
      live dialog could be held still long enough to show it.
    -->
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Ui/DialogInstances.cs" />
    <!--
      The panel layer's pure half: the window-to-panel classification and the element ids, roles, states and action
      choice. The Windows side of it (the probe, the reader, the surface) stays under Ui/ in files only the CLI
      project compiles.
    -->
    <!--
      The control type table is what every element read filters on, and one number in it was wrong for a whole
      release without anybody noticing: `ListItem` was written as 50029, which is `DataItem`, so no list row of
      Dorico's Setup lists or of the 繁衍声部格式 dialog ever reached a payload. It is pure data and pure rules, so it
      is asserted here - that the ids are the client interface definitions' and that the actionable set is the one
      that was measured.
    -->
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Ui/ControlTypes.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Ui/PanelModel.cs" />
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Ui/PanelCatalogue.cs" />
    <!--
      The execution verdict is not pure in the same way - it reads a file and waits for it - but it needs no Dorico
      and no Windows: the check points it at a fixture log, hands the verdict a wait of its own, and gains Dorico's
      lines on the polls it names. That is the only way to measure the retry and the offset rule without a live
      modal dialog, which no check can raise - and writing those lines from another thread instead, against a fixed
      sleep, is a race that was measured losing.
    -->
    <Compile Include="../../src/DoricoUse.Cli/Ui/LogQueue.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    B1: UnsafeSurface answers one question - is the scripting layer exposed? - from a compile-time constant, so
    the check compiles its source by hand like the files above, and can be asked to compile it the other way.
    The switch is passed on the command line (-p:ExposeUnsafeScriptRunApi=true), the same switch the CLI project
    takes; defining it here as well is what lets one Program.cs assert both directions, and is the only reason
    ci.yml builds this project twice: once as an ordinary build, once with the surface exposed.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ExposeUnsafeScriptRunApi Condition="'$(ExposeUnsafeScriptRunApi)' == 'true'">true</ExposeUnsafeScriptRunApi>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(ExposeUnsafeScriptRunApi)' == 'true'">$(DefineConstants);EXPOSE_UNSAFE_SCRIPT_RUN_API</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
